IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mva-dates`
IMPORT `mva-core`

-- Motor Vehicles Act 1959 (SA), Part 4 -- THE CLAIMANT'S SEAT: the person injured in a motor accident, or the person
-- claiming for a death, who brings a compulsory third party (CTP) claim. Every question is asked as that person would
-- ask it, from facts that person can state; the court's and the insurer's judgments are asked as conditions. The rules
-- are in mva-core.l4; this module restates none of them.
--
-- Based on content from the South Australian Legislation website, as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025 (unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See
-- NOTES.md.

§ `The claimant's questions`

§§ `Is it a CTP claim, and who answers it`

@export Does my death or injury claim count as caused by, or arising out of, the use of a motor vehicle under South Australia's compulsory third party (CTP) scheme (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 99(3), (3a), (4))?
GIVEN `how it came about` IS A `How the death or injury came about` @desc How the injury (or death) came about: as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle, of the vehicle running out of control, of a person travelling on a road colliding with the vehicle while it was stationary (or acting to avoid such a collision), or in some other way (for example while loading a parked vehicle). Bodily injury includes mental or nervous shock
      `it was a terrorist act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was caused by a terrorist act (as in the Terrorism (Commonwealth Powers) Act 2002)
      `the kind of vehicle` IS A `The kind of vehicle` @desc Whether the vehicle was a conditionally registered tractor, agricultural machine, mobile fork lift or self-propelled lawn care machine, or any other motor vehicle
      `the vehicle was on a road` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the vehicle was on a road (an area open to or used by the public and developed for, or mainly used for, driving motor vehicles) when it happened
GIVETH A `Use of a motor vehicle answer`
`Does my injury count as caused by the use of a motor vehicle` MEANS
    `the use of a motor vehicle test (s 99(3), (3a), (4))` `how it came about` `it was a terrorist act` `the kind of vehicle` `the vehicle was on a road`

@export Was the vehicle that injured me covered by a South Australian CTP policy on the day of the accident, counting the 30-day period of grace after registration ends (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 99A(8), (10))?
GIVEN `the authority` IS A `The vehicle's authority` @desc What allowed the vehicle on the road: its registration (trade plates count as registration), an exemption from registration, or a permit
      `the day it took effect` IS A DATE @desc The day that registration, exemption or permit took effect
      `the last day of its period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period for which it was granted (the registration expiry date)
      `it was renewed or granted again` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the registration was renewed, or a new registration, exemption or permit granted, after that period ended
      `the day the renewal took effect` IS A DATE @desc The day the renewal or new grant took effect (any day if there was none)
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
GIVETH A `Policy in force answer`
`Was the vehicle insured on the day of the accident` MEANS
    `was a CTP policy in force (s 99A(8))` `the authority` `the day it took effect` `the last day of its period` `it was renewed or granted again` `the day the renewal took effect` `the accident day`

@export Who answers my CTP claim for death or bodily injury in South Australia: the vehicle's CTP insurer, the nominal defendant (for an unidentified or uninsured vehicle, or a failed insurer), or nobody under this Act (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 ss 99, 115, 116, 118A)?
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `how it came about` IS A `How the death or injury came about` @desc How the injury (or death) came about: as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle, of it running out of control, of a person travelling on a road colliding with it while stationary (or avoiding that), or in some other way
      `it was a terrorist act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was caused by a terrorist act
      `the kind of vehicle` IS A `The kind of vehicle` @desc Whether the vehicle was a conditionally registered tractor, agricultural machine, mobile fork lift or self-propelled lawn care machine, or any other motor vehicle
      `the vehicle was on a road` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the vehicle was on a road when it happened
      `the vehicle's insurance` IS A `The vehicle's insurance` @desc The vehicle's insurance on the day: a South Australian CTP policy; a CTP policy under another State's or Territory's law that covers its use in South Australia; a public liability or other policy the Act accepts instead (some farm machines, vehicles exempted by regulation); none at all; or the vehicle could not be identified after due inquiry and search
      `the insurer has been declared under s 118A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Governor has declared, under s 118A, that the vehicle's CTP insurer is being wound up or in a compromise with its creditors with insufficient assets (rare)
GIVETH A `Who answers the claim`
`Who answers my claim` MEANS
    `who answers the claim` `the accident day` (`the use of a motor vehicle test (s 99(3), (3a), (4))` `how it came about` `it was a terrorist act` `the kind of vehicle` `the vehicle was on a road`) `the vehicle's insurance` `the vehicle was on a road` `the insurer has been declared under s 118A`

@export I was hurt in a road race by another participant: must the CTP policy cover it (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 104(2))?
GIVEN `I was a participant in a road race` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a driver, navigator or passenger in a vehicle taking part in a road race (a speed, reliability, skill or endurance contest declared under s 33 of the Road Traffic Act 1961 or held on a race track)
      `another participant caused it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your injury was caused by an act or omission of another participant in the race
GIVETH A `Road race answer`
`Must the policy cover my road race injury` MEANS
    `the road race exception (s 104(2))` `I was a participant in a road race` `another participant caused it`

DECLARE `Suing the uninsured driver answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: your action lies against the nominal defendant, and no action may be brought or continued against the uninsured owner or driver (s 116(3)(b))`
    `s 116(3)(b) does not stop it: your claim is not against the nominal defendant for an uninsured vehicle`

@export Can I sue the owner or driver of the uninsured vehicle myself, instead of the nominal defendant (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 116(3)(b))?
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `how it came about` IS A `How the death or injury came about` @desc How the injury (or death) came about
      `it was a terrorist act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was caused by a terrorist act
      `the kind of vehicle` IS A `The kind of vehicle` @desc Whether the vehicle was a conditionally registered farm or lawn machine or fork lift, or any other motor vehicle
      `the vehicle was on a road` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the vehicle was on a road when it happened
      `the vehicle's insurance` IS A `The vehicle's insurance` @desc The vehicle's insurance on the day (choose none at all for an uninsured vehicle)
GIVETH A `Suing the uninsured driver answer`
`Can I sue the uninsured driver` MEANS
    IF `the uninsured owner or driver cannot be sued (s 116(3)(b))` (`who answers the claim` `the accident day` (`the use of a motor vehicle test (s 99(3), (3a), (4))` `how it came about` `it was a terrorist act` `the kind of vehicle` `the vehicle was on a road`) `the vehicle's insurance` `the vehicle was on a road` FALSE)
    THEN `no: your action lies against the nominal defendant, and no action may be brought or continued against the uninsured owner or driver (s 116(3)(b))`
    ELSE `s 116(3)(b) does not stop it: your claim is not against the nominal defendant for an uninsured vehicle`

§§ `The notice of claim`

@export By when must I give the CTP insurer or the nominal defendant my notice of claim (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 126A(2)(a); Third Party Insurance Regulations reg 4(1))?
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `how it came about` IS A `How the death or injury came about` @desc How the injury (or death) came about
      `it was a terrorist act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was caused by a terrorist act
      `the kind of vehicle` IS A `The kind of vehicle` @desc Whether the vehicle was a conditionally registered farm or lawn machine or fork lift, or any other motor vehicle
      `the vehicle was on a road` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the vehicle was on a road when it happened
      `the vehicle's insurance` IS A `The vehicle's insurance` @desc The vehicle's insurance on the day
      `the insurer has been declared under s 118A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Governor has declared under s 118A that the vehicle's CTP insurer has failed (rare)
      `the day it became apparent` IS A DATE @desc For an unidentified or uninsured vehicle: the day it became apparent that the vehicle could not readily be identified, or was uninsured (any day otherwise)
GIVETH A `Notice deadline`
`By when must I give my notice of claim` MEANS
    `the time for the notice of claim (s 126A(2)(a), reg 4(1))` (`who answers the claim` `the accident day` (`the use of a motor vehicle test (s 99(3), (3a), (4))` `how it came about` `it was a terrorist act` `the kind of vehicle` `the vehicle was on a road`) `the vehicle's insurance` `the vehicle was on a road` `the insurer has been declared under s 118A`) `the accident day` `the day it became apparent`

@export What must my CTP notice of claim include or be accompanied by (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 126A(2); Third Party Insurance Regulations regs 5, 6)?
GIVEN `a police report was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the accident was reported to a police officer under the Road Traffic Act 1961 (then its report number is needed)
      `I claim loss of income or earning capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim includes loss of income or earning capacity
      `I claim medical expenses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim includes payment of medical expenses
      `it is a claim for a death under Part 5 of the Civil Liability Act 1936` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim relates to a person's death and is brought under Part 5 of the Civil Liability Act 1936 (a claim by the family)
      `I was driving a motor vehicle when injured` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were driving a motor vehicle when you were injured
GIVETH A LIST OF `Notice item`
`What must my notice of claim include` MEANS
    `what the notice of claim must include (s 126A(2), regs 5, 6)` `a police report was made` `I claim loss of income or earning capacity` `I claim medical expenses` `it is a claim for a death under Part 5 of the Civil Liability Act 1936` `I was driving a motor vehicle when injured`

DECLARE `My notice answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: s 126A(6) is not attracted by its timing`
    `not yet given, and there is still time`
    `late, but excused by reg 4(2): s 126A(6) is not attracted`
    `late and not excused: the insurer or nominal defendant may decline to consider or deal with the claim while the failure continues (s 126A(6)(a)), but you may still start or continue proceedings once the s 126A(2)(c) and (d) material is provided (reg 4(3))`
    `no notice under s 126A: there is no CTP claim under this Part`
    `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`

@export Did I give my CTP notice of claim in time, and if not, what follows (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 126A(6); Third Party Insurance Regulations reg 4)?
GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `how it came about` IS A `How the death or injury came about` @desc How the injury (or death) came about
      `it was a terrorist act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was caused by a terrorist act
      `the kind of vehicle` IS A `The kind of vehicle` @desc Whether the vehicle was a conditionally registered farm or lawn machine or fork lift, or any other motor vehicle
      `the vehicle was on a road` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the vehicle was on a road when it happened
      `the vehicle's insurance` IS A `The vehicle's insurance` @desc The vehicle's insurance on the day
      `the insurer has been declared under s 118A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Governor has declared under s 118A that the vehicle's CTP insurer has failed (rare)
      `I have given the notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given a notice of claim that complies with s 126A(2)
      `the day I gave it` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave it (any day if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `it was or can still be given as soon as reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an unidentified or uninsured vehicle only: whether the notice was (or, if not yet given, can still be) given as soon as reasonably practicable after it became apparent the vehicle could not be identified or was uninsured
      `the cause of any lateness` IS A `Cause of lateness` @desc If the notice was late: whether the lateness was caused by your ignorance or mistake, your absence from the State, your inability because of the injury or a legal disability, or another reasonable cause, or none of these
      `the proper assessment of the claim has been substantially prejudiced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the notice was late: whether the delay has substantially prejudiced the proper assessment of the claim
GIVETH A `My notice answer`
`Did I give my notice of claim in time` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the accident is within this encoding` `the accident day`)
    THEN `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`
    ELSE IF NOT (`there is a CTP claim under this Part` `the claim`)
         THEN `no notice under s 126A: there is no CTP claim under this Part`
         ELSE CONSIDER `what became of the notice duty`
              WHEN `the duty was kept in time` THEN `in time: s 126A(6) is not attracted by its timing`
              WHEN `the time has not yet run out` THEN `not yet given, and there is still time`
              WHEN `the duty was not kept in time` THEN
                  IF `the lateness is excused (reg 4(2))` `the cause of any lateness` `the proper assessment of the claim has been substantially prejudiced`
                  THEN `late, but excused by reg 4(2): s 126A(6) is not attracted`
                  ELSE `late and not excused: the insurer or nominal defendant may decline to consider or deal with the claim while the failure continues (s 126A(6)(a)), but you may still start or continue proceedings once the s 126A(2)(c) and (d) material is provided (reg 4(3))`
    WHERE
        `the claim` MEANS `who answers the claim` `the accident day` (`the use of a motor vehicle test (s 99(3), (3a), (4))` `how it came about` `it was a terrorist act` `the kind of vehicle` `the vehicle was on a road`) `the vehicle's insurance` `the vehicle was on a road` `the insurer has been declared under s 118A`
        `the days to the last day` MEANS `the days from` `the accident day` `to` (`the accident day` `plus months` `the months for the notice of claim (reg 4(1)(b))`)
        `what became of the notice duty` MEANS
            IF     `the claim` EQUALS `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was not identified (s 115)`
                OR `the claim` EQUALS `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was uninsured (s 116)`
            THEN IF `it was or can still be given as soon as reasonably practicable`
                 THEN IF `I have given the notice` THEN `the duty was kept in time` ELSE `the time has not yet run out`
                 ELSE `the duty was not kept in time`
            ELSE `what became of` (`s 126A(1), reg 4(1)(b) -- the claimant must furnish a complying notice of claim within 6 months after the accident` `the insurer` `the days to the last day`) `for` `the claimant` `doing` `furnish a notice of claim complying with s 126A(2)` `done` `I have given the notice` `at` (`the days from` `the accident day` `to` `the day I gave it`) `now` (`the days from` `the accident day` `to` `today`)

@export May I start, or carry on, court proceedings on my CTP claim as far as the notice of claim rules go (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 126A(6)(b); Third Party Insurance Regulations reg 4(3))?
GIVEN `I have provided the required statement, medical certificate, police report number, other prescribed material and access authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have provided everything s 126A(2)(c) and (d) requires: the statement of the claim's details, the medical certificate or opinion, the police report number (if any), the evidence regulation 5 prescribes, and the signed authority for the insurer to obtain records
GIVETH A `Proceedings answer`
`May I start court proceedings` MEANS
    `may proceedings be started or continued (s 126A(6)(b), reg 4(3))` `I have provided the required statement, medical certificate, police report number, other prescribed material and access authority`

@export By when must the CTP insurer (or the nominal defendant) send me a copy of a record or information it obtained with my signed authority (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 126A(4))?
GIVEN `the day it received the record` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer or nominal defendant received the record or information under your access authority
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the insurer send me a copy` MEANS
    `the day it received the record` `plus days` `the days to send the claimant a copy (s 126A(4))`

@export Could my action against the nominal defendant be dismissed because I was slow to give it notice of my claim (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 115(3), (4), s 116(4), (5))?
GIVEN `I gave notice as soon as reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the nominal defendant written notice of your claim, with a short statement of its grounds, as soon as reasonably practicable after it became apparent the vehicle could not be identified or was uninsured
      `the court is satisfied the nominal defendant was prejudiced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the late notice prejudiced the nominal defendant in conducting its defence
GIVETH A `Dismissal answer`
`Could my action against the nominal defendant be dismissed for late notice` MEANS
    `may the action against the nominal defendant be dismissed for late notice (s 115(4), s 116(5))` `I gave notice as soon as reasonably practicable` `the court is satisfied the nominal defendant was prejudiced`

§§ `Recovering from the insurer`

@export Can I recover my judgment or claim directly from the at-fault driver's CTP insurer (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 ss 112, 113)? (Situation: you have an unpaid judgment, or the insured person is dead or cannot be served.)
GIVEN `my situation` IS A `Direct action situation` @desc Whether you have a judgment against the insured owner or driver that has not been paid, or the insured person who is liable is dead or cannot be served with court documents, or neither
      `the insurer knew of my action before it came on for hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an unpaid judgment: whether the insurer knew, before the action came on for hearing, that you had started it
      `I gave the insurer notice in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a dead or unservable insured person: whether you gave the insurer notice of your claim and a short statement of its grounds as soon as possible after learning that, or in time to keep the insurer from being prejudiced
GIVETH A `Direct action answer`
`Can I recover from the insurer directly` MEANS
    `may the claimant recover from the insurer directly (ss 112, 113)` `my situation` `the insurer knew of my action before it came on for hearing` `I gave the insurer notice in time`

@export In my direct action against the CTP insurer, can it refuse to pay because the policy was obtained by a misstatement, or because the insured person broke the policy or the Act (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 114)?
GIVEN `the insurer's ground` IS A `Insurer's ground` @desc The ground the insurer relies on: the policy was obtained by mis-statement or non-disclosure; the insured person broke a term, condition or warranty of the policy or a provision of Part 4; or some other ground
GIVETH A `Section 114 answer`
`Can the insurer refuse on this ground` MEANS
    `is it a defence to a direct action (s 114)` `the insurer's ground`

DECLARE `Damages kind answer` IS ONE OF
    `the CTP insurer covers the insured person's liability for it (s 104(1)(a))`
    `no: the CTP insurer is not liable to pay aggravated, exemplary or punitive damages; they fall on the person who was ordered to pay them (s 113A)`

@export Will the CTP insurer pay this kind of damages awarded to me (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 113A)?
GIVEN `the kind of damages` IS A `Kind of damages` @desc Compensatory damages for the death or bodily injury, aggravated damages, or exemplary or punitive damages
GIVETH A `Damages kind answer`
`Will the insurer pay this kind of damages` MEANS
    IF `the insurer is barred from paying it (s 113A)` `the kind of damages`
    THEN `no: the CTP insurer is not liable to pay aggravated, exemplary or punitive damages; they fall on the person who was ordered to pay them (s 113A)`
    ELSE `the CTP insurer covers the insured person's liability for it (s 104(1)(a))`

@export How much are my CTP damages once the expenses the insurer has already paid are taken off (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 124AC)?
GIVEN `my damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages for the death or bodily injury, in dollars, before this credit
      `the expenses the insurer has paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The expenses you claimed (for example medical bills) that the insurer has paid to you or on your behalf, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much are my damages after the expenses paid` MEANS
    `the damages after the s 124AC credit` `my damages` `the expenses the insurer has paid`

§§ `Medical information for the insurer`

@export By when must I tell the CTP insurer the name of a doctor I consulted about my injury and the day I saw them (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 127(2)(b))?
GIVEN `the day I consulted the doctor` IS A DATE @desc The day you consulted a medical practitioner about the injury you claim for
      `extra days allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc Extra days beyond 21 that the insurer has allowed you, or that are reasonable in the circumstances (0 if none)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I tell the insurer about my doctor` MEANS
    `the day I consulted the doctor` `plus days` (`the days for the medical notices (s 127(2)(b), (c))` PLUS `extra days allowed`)

@export By when must I send the CTP insurer a copy of a written medical report about my injury (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 127(2)(c))?
GIVEN `the day I received the report` IS A DATE @desc The day you (or your lawyer) received a written report from a medical practitioner you consulted about the injury
      `extra days allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc Extra days beyond 21 that are reasonable in the circumstances (0 if none)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I send the insurer the report` MEANS
    `the day I received the report` `plus days` (`the days for the medical notices (s 127(2)(b), (c))` PLUS `extra days allowed`)

DECLARE `My medical duty answer` IS ONE OF
    `you kept it in time`
    `there is still time`
    `late, but s 127(6) has no effect until proceedings have been commenced`
    `late, but s 127(6) does not apply: the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports (s 127(6a))`
    `late: the court may award costs against you and may take the failure into account in assessing your compensation (s 127(6), (7))`

@export Have I kept my duty to tell the CTP insurer about a doctor's consultation, or to send it a medical report, in time, and what can follow if not (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 127(2), (6), (6a))?
GIVEN `the duty` IS A `Medical duty` @desc Which duty: informing the insurer of a consultation (s 127(2)(b)), or sending it a copy of a medical report (s 127(2)(c))
      `the day it started` IS A DATE @desc The day of the consultation, or the day you received the report
      `extra days allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc Extra days beyond 21 the insurer allowed or that are reasonable in the circumstances (0 if none)
      `I have done it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have told the insurer in writing, or sent the copy
      `the day I did it` IS A DATE @desc The day you did (any day if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `court proceedings have been commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether proceedings on your claim have been commenced in a court
      `the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a report: whether you dealt with it under court rules that relieve a party from disclosing a medical report that would unfairly prejudice its case
GIVETH A `My medical duty answer`
`Have I kept my medical information duty` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the outcome`
    WHEN `the duty was kept in time` THEN `you kept it in time`
    WHEN `the time has not yet run out` THEN `there is still time`
    WHEN `the duty was not kept in time` THEN
        CONSIDER `what follows a failure of a s 127(2) duty` `the duty` FALSE `court proceedings have been commenced` `the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports`
        WHEN `no consequence under s 127(6) yet: it applies once proceedings have been commenced before a court` THEN `late, but s 127(6) has no effect until proceedings have been commenced`
        WHEN `no consequence under s 127(6): the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports (s 127(6a))` THEN `late, but s 127(6) does not apply: the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports (s 127(6a))`
        OTHERWISE `late: the court may award costs against you and may take the failure into account in assessing your compensation (s 127(6), (7))`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days for the medical notices (s 127(2)(b), (c))` PLUS `extra days allowed`
        `the done time` MEANS `the days from` `the day it started` `to` `the day I did it`
        `the now time` MEANS `the days from` `the day it started` `to` `today`
        `the outcome` MEANS
            IF `the duty` EQUALS `informing the insurer of a consultation (s 127(2)(b))`
            THEN `what became of` (`s 127(2)(b) -- the claimant must inform the insurer of a consultation within 21 days` `the days allowed`) `for` `the claimant` `doing` `inform the insurer in writing of the medical practitioner consulted and the day of the consultation` `done` `I have done it` `at` `the done time` `now` `the now time`
            ELSE `what became of` (`s 127(2)(c) -- the claimant must send the insurer a copy of a medical report within 21 days of receiving it` `the days allowed`) `for` `the claimant` `doing` `send the insurer a copy of the medical report` `done` `I have done it` `at` `the done time` `now` `the now time`

§§ `A child's treatment, care and support (only if the injured person was under 16)`

@export My child was hurt in a motor accident before turning 16: must the CTP insurer pay the child's treatment, care and support costs, whoever was at fault (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 127B)?
GIVEN `the child's age on the day of the accident` IS A NUMBER @desc The injured child's age in whole years on the day of the accident
      `it happened in South Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident that caused the injury happened in South Australia
      `the child is a Lifetime Support Scheme participant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child is a participant in the Lifetime Support Scheme (for very serious injuries such as spinal cord or brain injury)
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
      `how it came about` IS A `How the death or injury came about` @desc How the injury came about
      `it was a terrorist act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury was caused by a terrorist act
      `the kind of vehicle` IS A `The kind of vehicle` @desc Whether the vehicle was a conditionally registered farm or lawn machine or fork lift, or any other motor vehicle
      `the vehicle was on a road` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the vehicle was on a road when it happened
      `the vehicle's insurance` IS A `The vehicle's insurance` @desc The vehicle's insurance on the day
GIVETH A `Section 127B answer`
`Must the insurer pay my child's treatment and care` MEANS
    `must the insurer pay the child's treatment, care and support (s 127B)` `the child's age on the day of the accident` `it happened in South Australia` `the child is a Lifetime Support Scheme participant` (`who answers the claim` `the accident day` (`the use of a motor vehicle test (s 99(3), (3a), (4))` `how it came about` `it was a terrorist act` `the kind of vehicle` `the vehicle was on a road`) `the vehicle's insurance` `the vehicle was on a road` FALSE) `the accident day`

§§ `Legal costs and who may act (only if the claim goes to court)`

@export If my CTP claim goes to court, can the court order my legal costs to be paid, given the amount I recover (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 127C(1), (3))?
GIVEN `the total amount I recover` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount recovered on the claim, in dollars
      `the court finds exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds exceptional circumstances justifying the costs
      `it is to approve a settlement for a person under a legal disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are to obtain the court's approval of a compromise or settlement for a person under a legal disability (such as a child)
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`Can my legal costs be paid` MEANS
    `the claimant's costs (s 127C(1), (3))` `the total amount I recover` `the court finds exceptional circumstances` `it is to approve a settlement for a person under a legal disability` `the accident day`

@export If my CTP claim goes to court, how far can I be ordered to pay the defendant's legal costs, given the amount I claim (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 127C(2))?
GIVEN `the total amount I claim` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount claimed, in dollars
      `the court finds exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds exceptional circumstances justifying the costs
      `the accident day` IS A DATE @desc The day of the accident
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`Can I be made to pay the defendant's costs` MEANS
    `the defendant's costs (s 127C(2))` `the total amount I claim` `the court finds exceptional circumstances` `the accident day`

@export Is an agreement in which I gave up my right to claim for injury from a motor vehicle binding on me (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 133)?
GIVEN `what the right is about` IS A `What the right is about` @desc Whether the right given up is a right about death or bodily injury, or about loss of or damage to property
      `I agreed before the injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement was made in advance, before the death or injury happened (for example in a ride or hire agreement), rather than afterwards (such as a settlement)
GIVETH A `Contracting out answer`
`Is my agreement giving up my claim binding` MEANS
    `is the agreement giving up the right void (s 133)` `what the right is about` `I agreed before the injury`

@export May this person act on my motor accident claim for a fee or other reward (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 134)?
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who would act` @desc Who would act: a qualified legal practitioner in the course of their profession; an officer of an employees' association (a union) acting for you as a member; a person employed or instructed by an approved insurer; or anyone else (such as a claims agent)
      `for a fee or reward` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they would act for, or expecting, a fee or other reward
GIVETH A `Acting for reward answer`
`May this person act on my claim for a fee` MEANS
    `may this person act on the claim for reward (s 134)` `who` `for a fee or reward`

@export If I am found guilty of giving false or misleading information about my motor accident claim, what can be recovered from me (Motor Vehicles Act 1959 s 124(6), (6a), (6b))?
GIVEN `I have been paid on the claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an amount has been paid to you in connection with the claim
      `I have been found guilty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been found guilty of giving false or misleading notice or information under s 124(6) or (6a) in connection with the claim (maximum penalty $50 000 or imprisonment for one year)
      `the financial benefit I gained` IS A NUMBER @desc The financial benefit, in dollars, you gained from the offence
GIVETH A `False information recovery answer`
`What can be recovered from me for false information` MEANS
    `what can be recovered for false information (s 124(6b))` `I have been paid on the claim` `I have been found guilty` `the financial benefit I gained`
